AI WEB ANNOTATOR(1)

NAME

    ai-web-annotator - annotate web pages and export context for AI tools

SYNOPSIS

    AI Web Annotator is used through its Chrome extension interface.

DESCRIPTION

    AI Web Annotator is a Chrome extension for interactively annotating
    elements on web pages.

    Selected elements can be associated with comments and numbered
    annotations. When an annotation session is complete, the extension
    exports an annotated screenshot, structured DOM information, and a
    formatted prompt for use with AI tools.

REQUIREMENTS

    Node.js 20 or later
    npm
    Google Chrome or another Chromium-based browser

    Docker is optional.

INSTALLATION

    Install dependencies:

        npm install

    Build the extension:

        npm run build

    Open chrome://extensions/ in Chrome, enable Developer mode, select
    Load unpacked, and choose the dist/ directory.

USAGE

    Open a web page and click the AI Web Annotator extension icon. The
    side panel opens beside the page: the page is never covered or resized
    by the extension.

    Select "Ajouter une annotation" (key A) to enter annotation mode, then
    click elements to place numbered annotations. Type the comment in the
    panel; the type (Style, Bug, Texte) is suggested and vague comments are
    flagged. Several annotations can be placed in a row. Select "Terminer
    l'annotation" (Escape) to browse freely again: open menus, panels and
    dialogs. When the page changes (dialog, menu, route...), the annotated
    screen is saved and a new screen starts automatically.

    "Exporter la session" opens the export view in the panel:

        An annotated PNG screenshot per screen.
        Structured DOM context in JSON format.
        CSS selectors and XPath expressions.
        A formatted prompt (AI agent, GitHub issue or JSON) copied to the
        clipboard.

DEVELOPMENT

    Start development mode:

        npm run dev

    Create a distributable archive:

        npm run package

    Run using Docker:

        docker compose up

TESTING

    Unit tests (jsdom):

        npm test

    End-to-end tests drive a real Chromium with the extension loaded
    (service worker, content script, side panel page, screenshots,
    clipboard, session restore across navigations):

        npx playwright install chromium
        npm run test:e2e

    All tests should pass before submitting changes.

IMAGE HOSTING

    Screenshots can be uploaded to your own S3-compatible storage so the
    prompt, the GitHub issue and the JSON carry image links the AI can
    open. Works with Garage, MinIO, Cloudflare R2 or any S3 server
    (path-style, AWS Signature V4).

    Self-hosted example with Garage:

        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml up -d
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage status
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage layout assign -z dc1 -c 1G <node-id>
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage layout apply --version 1
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage bucket create annotations
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage key create annotator
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage bucket allow --read --write --owner annotations --key annotator
        docker compose -f compose.storage.yaml exec garage /garage bucket website --allow annotations

    Then open "Hébergement des images" in the side panel and fill in:
    endpoint (S3 API URL), region (garage), bucket, access key, secret key,
    public URL (the web endpoint, e.g. https://annotations.img.example.cloud)
    and the link lifetime. Files are stored under annotations/<random>/ so
    URLs cannot be guessed, and an expiration rule is installed on the
    bucket. The secret stays in the browser (chrome.storage.local).

JEV (DEVELOPMENT-TIME DECISIONS)

    Jev (TypeSafe System One) is used while developing, never inside the
    extension. Put TYPESAFE_API_KEY in .env (ignored by git), then:

        npm run jev -- noul "Is this a flaky test?" --state "..."
        npm run jev -- choice "Which fits?" --option a="..." --option b="..." --state "..."
        npm run eval:heuristics               # Jev judges a corpus
        npm run eval:heuristics -- --heldout  # corpus not used for tuning
        npm run eval:heuristics -- --offline  # reuse recorded judgments

    The extension's local rules (annotation type, "too vague" nudge,
    "is this DOM change a new screen") are measured against Jev judgments
    recorded in test/fixtures; npm test checks the agreement offline.

